Not in your sources — from general knowledge: The most modern and recommended way to center a `div` horizontally is using **Flexbox**.

### Method 1: Flexbox (Recommended)
Apply these styles to the **parent** container of the div you want to center.

```css
.parent {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center; /* Centers children horizontally */
}
```

If you also need it centered vertically within the parent, add `align-items: center`.

---

### Method 2: Margin Auto (Block Level)
This works if the child element has a defined width. Apply this directly to the **child** div.

```css
.child {
  margin-left: auto;
  margin-right: auto;
  width: fit-content; /* Or a specific pixel/percentage value */
}
```

*Note: In modern browsers, simply using `margin-inline: auto` on the child is often sufficient.*

---

### Method 3: Text Align (Quick & Dirty)
If you only care about inline content inside the parent and don't want to use Flexbox/Grid, you can center text alignment in the parent. This works for `<div>`s containing inline elements or other block-level elements if they are treated as inline-blocks.

```css
.parent {
  text-align: center;
}
.child {
  display: inline-block; /* Required for this method to work reliably */
}
```
